# The domain-profile system [SPEC:FL-PROFILES]

> The format knows that domains exist. It knows nothing about any particular domain.
> Everything a knowledge format is normally tempted to hardcode — what `REQ-` means, which
> edge types are legal, which properties are required — is registered by a profile that
> tooling loads at runtime.

---

## [PROFILE-CONTRACT] What a profile registers

- part-of: [FL-PROFILES]
- depends-on: [MODEL-ID]
- see-also: [PROFILE-SELFHOST]
- status: draft
- importance: high

A domain profile is a document that declares a domain's vocabulary and how its corpus is
meant to be traversed:

| Registers | Effect |
|-----------|--------|
| Namespaces | Edge types under those namespaces stop being flagged as unregistered |
| Id prefixes | `REQ-`, `MOD-`, `TEST-` acquire documented meaning and become a taxonomy |
| Edge types | Domain relationships, each assigned to one of the four core layers |
| Properties | Domain keys, their types, and which are required on which node kinds |
| Document tags | The doctypes this domain's documents may carry |
| Traversal policy | Default entry points, named focus areas, keyword triggers |

The engine core consumes exactly one hook from a profile today: the set of registered
namespaces. Everything else in the profile is read by higher layers — linters, authoring
agents, documentation renderers. That is a deliberate staging, and it is worth being
plain about it: **profile-driven validation of prefixes, required properties, enum values
and more is implemented in the linter at warning severity, but runs only when a
machine-readable profile is in scope, and the shipped reference profiles do not provide
one.** With them, a profile is still documentation plus a namespace registration.

---

## [PROFILE-NAMESPACE] One deconfliction mechanism, applied everywhere

- part-of: [FL-PROFILES]
- depends-on: [PROFILE-CONTRACT]
- status: draft
- importance: high

Namespaces deconflict every extensibility surface, with no per-surface special case:

| Surface | Core | Domain |
|---------|------|--------|
| Edge types | `DEPENDS_ON` | `clinical.CONTRAINDICATES` |
| Id prefixes | — | `REQ-`, `PT-`, `MOD-` |
| Properties | `status`, `confidence` | `dosage`, `contract-version` |
| Document tags | `SPEC`, `SYSTEM` | `RUNBOOK`, `CARE-PLAN` |
| Cross-corpus refs | — | `[ops.acme:SPEC:DEPLOY]` |

`clinical.STATUS` and `swe.STATUS` coexist without collision because they are different
names, not because anything arbitrates between them. There is no conflict-resolution
protocol to specify, which is the point of choosing namespacing over a central registry.

---

## [PROFILE-TIERS] Three tiers — core, registered, private

- part-of: [FL-PROFILES]
- depends-on: [PROFILE-NAMESPACE]
- see-also: [PROFILE-PROMOTION]
- status: draft

| Tier | Form | Guarantee |
|------|------|-----------|
| Core | `DEPENDS_ON`, `core.DEPENDS_ON` | Immutable, ships with the format, every parser understands it |
| Registered | `clinical.CONTRAINDICATES` | Published in a profile; parsers load it on demand |
| Private | a bare non-core type | Local use, no registration, no collision guarantee |

A parser meeting an unregistered namespace **preserves the edge and flags it**. It does not
drop it and it does not fail. That behaviour is what makes the tiers usable in practice: a
consumer who has not loaded your profile still gets a complete graph, with the parts they
cannot validate marked. Zero friction to read, full validation to author.

---

## [PROFILE-BOUNDARY] The core/domain boundary test

- part-of: [FL-PROFILES]
- see-also: [PROFILE-CONTRACT]
- status: draft
- importance: high

Every proposed feature answers one question: does it define a **structural mechanism** — an
interface, a contract, a syntax rule — or a **specific implementation** — a tool, a
workflow, a vocabulary?

| Core | Domain |
|------|--------|
| The confidence attribute exists and is a number in `[0.0, 1.0]` | How a clinical domain assigns confidence |
| The four graph layers | Which edge types a domain places in each |
| `[PREFIX-NAME]` is a reference | What `PT-` means |
| A governance mechanism exists | A given organization's change-approval workflow |
| An event-stream contract | The specific event types a domain emits |

This is the test that keeps the format multi-domain rather than one domain claiming
universality. It is applied to every amendment, and the answer is recorded.

---

## [PROFILE-PROMOTION] Frequency-driven promotion

- part-of: [FL-PROFILES]
- depends-on: [PROFILE-TIERS]
- see-also: [MODEL-CHANGE]
- status: draft

Core vocabulary is not designed up front. It is promoted from observed convergence.

| Domains using it | Action |
|------------------|--------|
| 1 | Domain-specific. No action. |
| 2 | Noted in the registry. No promotion. |
| 3 or more, compatible semantics | Candidate: promote to core, or mint an intermediate namespace for the cluster |
| 3 or more, incompatible semantics | No action — namespaces already deconflict them |

The intermediate-namespace option matters more than it looks. A concept shared by a cluster
of domains but irrelevant to the rest belongs in its own namespace, not in core. Core grows
only for genuinely universal structure, because every addition to core is something every
parser must implement forever.

Promotion to core requires a recorded `change` and `context_for_change` block with an
`invalidating_assumption`, per [MODEL-CHANGE]. A core amendment without a recorded premise
is not accepted.

---

## [PROFILE-SELFHOST] Profiles are themselves FactLattice documents

- part-of: [FL-PROFILES]
- see-also: [MODEL-DOCTYPE]
- status: draft

A domain profile is a `.lts` document parsed by the parser it configures. It is a node in
a graph, it can be traversed, linted, referenced, and diffed like any other document.

This is the same self-hosting property this corpus demonstrates: the specification of
FactLattice is written in FactLattice. It is not a rhetorical flourish — it is the cheapest
available test that the format is expressive enough for structured technical content,
because a format that could not carry its own specification would fail the test loudly and
immediately.

---

*FL-PROFILES — the extensibility mechanism. All domains are equal; none is the reference
implementation.*
